  <p class="note">This is a practical orientation, not legal advice or a substitute for inspecting the property. The Consumer Affairs Victoria links below are the authoritative starting point for current Victorian requirements.</p>

  <h2>The current baseline</h2>
  <p>Consumer Affairs Victoria says Victorian rental properties are subject to 15 categories of minimum standards. Since 25 November 2025, a rental property must meet the applicable minimum standards before it is advertised or offered for rent, as well as before a renter moves in.</p>
  <p>The categories cover the fundamentals of a safe and functional home: bathrooms, electrical safety, heating, kitchens, laundries, lighting, locks, mould and damp, structural soundness, toilets, ventilation, vermin-proof bins, windows, window coverings and window-covering anchors.</p>

  <h2>Why the agreement history still matters</h2>
  <p>The rules can apply differently to some older agreements. Consumer Affairs Victoria explains that the minimum standards apply to agreements that started on or after 29 March 2021, and to older agreements that became periodic on or after that date. If you have an older continuing arrangement, check the current government guidance rather than relying on a generic checklist.</p>

  <h2>What a rental provider can do now</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Use the current Consumer Affairs Victoria checklist to review the property before advertising or re-leasing.</li>
    <li>Keep useful evidence of checks, repairs, upgrades and qualified-trade work rather than relying on memory.</li>
    <li>Resolve obvious maintenance or safety issues before they become a rushed leasing problem.</li>
    <li>Ask how the property will be monitored during the tenancy, not only at the point of leasing.</li>
    <li>Plan for scheduled reforms early where the property is likely to need upgrades.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>More change is already scheduled</h2>
  <p>Consumer Affairs Victoria lists further rental-law changes scheduled for 13 October 2026, including stronger record-keeping expectations around minimum-standards compliance and broader two-year gas and electrical safety-check requirements.</p>
  <p>New minimum energy-efficiency standards are then scheduled to begin in phases from 1 March 2027. The government guidance covers six areas—heating, cooling, hot water, shower heads, ceiling insulation and draughtproofing—with different triggers and commencement dates. That means “2027 changes” should not be treated as one blanket replacement deadline.</p>

  <h2>The management question behind the checklist</h2>
  <p>Compliance matters, but owners also need a process for noticing, documenting and acting on property issues. A useful property-management relationship should make it easier to understand what has been checked, what still needs attention, who needs to be engaged and what the next decision is.</p>
  <p>That is different from promising that a property will always be “fully compliant.” Rules change, properties change and qualified trades or specialist advice may be required. The stronger standard is a documented, proactive process and clear communication when something needs action.</p>
